  {
    "episode_id": 81066,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81066.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think that that's kind of been a trend across the country for a long, long time, is that the cities, the urban areas are more Democrat than other areas, rural areas. And I think you're seeing some of that more in Cincinnati. It's interesting, you know, we've seen so many good things happen in Cincinnati. A lot of that has to do with the things that we have voted and kept in place as Republicans, such as new market tax credits and historic tax credits, which has been a big part of revamping over the Rhine. But, you know, it is what it is. The Hamlet County has definitely changed, but also I think the rural areas and other areas have gotten stronger Republican during that time.",
